{"name":"Genie","hex":"#3e4364","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Genie","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A male given name, diminutive of Eugene and pet form of Gene","A female given name, diminutive of Eugenie and Eugenia"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Genie"}],"entries":[{"title":"Genie (feral child)","blocks":[{"text":"Genie is the pseudonym of an American feral child who was a victim of severe abuse, neglect, and social isolation. Her circumstances are prominently recorded in the annals of linguistics and abnormal child psychology. When she was approximately 20 months old, her father began keeping her in a locked room. During this period, he almost always strapped her to a child's toilet or bound her in a crib with her arms and legs immobilized, forbade anyone to interact with her, provided her with almost no stimulation of any kind, and left her severely malnourished. The extent of her isolation prevented her from being exposed to any significant amount of speech, and as a result she did not acquire language during her childhood. Her abuse came to the attention of Los Angeles County child welfare authorities in November 1970, when she was 13 years and 7 months old, after which she became a ward of the state of California."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Genie_immediately_after_rescue.jpg/330px-Genie_immediately_after_rescue.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Genie_(feral_child)","wikidata":"Q237068"},{"title":"Genie (Disney)","blocks":[{"text":"The Genie is a fictional character who appeared in Walt Disney Pictures' animated film Aladdin (1992), later appearing in other media of the Aladdin franchise as one of its main characters, as well as throughout other Disney media; he was based on popular culture depictions of genies or djinns. He was voiced by Robin Williams in the first film, on whom the character's mannerisms were based. Following a contract dispute between Williams and Disney, Dan Castellaneta voiced the Genie in the direct-to-video feature The Return of Jafar, as well as the television series. Williams reprised the role for the final film installment Aladdin and the King of Thieves, and the character's own educational mini-series Great Minds Think for Themselves."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/0/0c/The_Genie_Aladdin.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Genie_(Disney)","wikidata":"Q2345667"},{"title":"Jinn","blocks":[{"text":"Jinn, also romanized as djinn or anglicized as genies, are supernatural beings in Islam, ancient Arabian religion and Arabic folklore and mythology. Like humans, they are accountable for their deeds and can be either believers (Mu'minun) or unbelievers (kuffar), depending on whether they accept God's guidance."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Shah_Namah%2C_the_Persian_Epic_of_the_Kings_Wellcome_L0035191.jpg/330px-Shah_Namah%2C_the_Persian_Epic_of_the_Kings_Wellcome_L0035191.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jinn","wikidata":"Q3465"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/f/f0/Genie_%28feral_child%29.jpg/250px-Genie_%28feral_child%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/f/f0/Genie_%28feral_child%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/f/f0/Genie_%28feral_child%29.jpg/250px-Genie_%28feral_child%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Genie in the hospital yard a few weeks after her admission, displaying her characteristic \"bunny walk\"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGenie_(feral_child).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Salk_Institute1.jpg/500px-Salk_Institute1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Salk_Institute1.jpg/1280px-Salk_Institute1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Salk_Institute1.jpg/500px-Salk_Institute1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Salk Institute for Biological Studies, where researchers analyzed the data from the first of several brain exams on Genie","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASalk_Institute1.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Victor_of_Aveyron%2C_1800.jpg/330px-Victor_of_Aveyron%2C_1800.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Victor_of_Aveyron%2C_1800.jpg/330px-Victor_of_Aveyron%2C_1800.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A lithograph of Victor of Aveyron c. 1800","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVictor_of_Aveyron%2C_1800.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/5/5f/Genie_working_with_Marilyn.JPG/250px-Genie_working_with_Marilyn.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/5/5f/Genie_working_with_Marilyn.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/5/5f/Genie_working_with_Marilyn.JPG/250px-Genie_working_with_Marilyn.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Genie while working with Marilyn Rigler","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A